 number of RAN plenary meeting, e.g. 3 times / year. 4. No RAN discussions in parallel to RAN WG meetings Nowadays, many WG delegates are also involved in RAN plenary meeting. Avoid overlapping is very important for the delegates life and WG progress. Here, reducing the number of RAN plenary meeting is also a solution. 5. Pragmatism The realistic TU estimation is most important. If a certain item is not finished within a release, this item should be down-scoped or postponed to the next release. The delayed item is not the fault of leadership but the fault of WI/SI rapporteur (i.e. wrong TU estimation). Huawei | On one hand some delegates are tired (those handling RAN discussions in addition to RAN2), but on the other hand it is not clear how we think RAN2 should be able to deliver on the technical work given by RAN if we start having less meetings and reduce the use of the tools that we have available to make progress. Minimize email discussions between meetings Long email discussions in RAN2 are useful and their outcome is the starting point for companies contributions, which then results in moderators summaries at the beginning of the e-meetings, which then get treated in the meeting. Before e-meetings era, the output of email discussions was going to be treated in RAN2 online, without the intermediate additional step of the moderators summaries. RAN2 Chairman added those moderators summaries, but those are based on companies submissions, available only 10 days before the meeting starts. The long email discussions have been a way a working for RAN2 for (I think) about 15 years. In our opinion a useful and good one. In fact delegates are not so busy in between meetings, especially if the meetings are 3 months apart. So first of all we need to keep them. Then of course we should not abuse them, so we can limit the number. But with no hard limit or so. It should be as usual session chairs discretion. Hard stops of GTW sessions: The hard stop is not clever if the session chair thinks that we are 5 minutes away from an agreement. On the other hand, no new discussions should be started once the stop time occurred. The chairman and all the session chairs are very well aware of it, so it’s a matter of execution, and some minimum flexibility should be tolerated when useful (5 – 10 minutes max) One e-meeting per quarter We think this is a RAN plenary discussion, not only for RAN2. We note that a few years ago the RAN2 meetings per year went down from 8 to 6. Bringing them from 6 to 4 would significantly impact the ability for RAN2 (and other WGs) to deliver on RAN approved WIs/SIs. We are positive on trying to limit the email discussions in between meetings occurring in the same quarter. Other than that, nothing else seems needed. No RAN discussions in parallel to RAN WG meetings This is also RAN level discussion. We agree that some delegates that had to handle the RAN Rel-18 discussions in addition to RAN2 have been working hard. In our opinion even more challenging was the work for RAN2 officials, in between chairing Rel18 discussions and RAN2 preparation. This was badly planned. Luckily this situation finished now and we also hope that it will not occur in the future. Pragmatism Another RAN plenary discussion. Postponing an almost complete WI to the next release will of course cause more overload. The current mechanism of Status Report to plenary allows scope trimming towards the end of the release in the rare cases where is needed. There is also the mechanism of exception sheets (which of course should not be abused). Better to keep it simple from the start with TUs planning, reasonable scope, and not pursue complicated solutions in due course, etc. Samsung | We, in general, share the view with many others that something 

